## The families, and the kinds that belong to them
#
# **A family is an abstract relation: columns and no rows.** The columns are the shape its members
# share, and `{member ...}` is the set - Sean, 2026-09-17, having considered an exists/not-exists
# trait and a set of kinds and asking whether both were relevant: they are the same mechanism.
# `spec/data/families.4x` writes it this way already, and `spec/data/for.4x` uses it for exactly
# what it is for here: `{for block:build-extractor seq:3 kind:$resource}`.

{column id:72 relation:unit seq:1 name:where}
{column id:73 relation:unit seq:2 name:quantity}
{column id:74 relation:resource seq:1 name:where}
{column id:75 relation:resource seq:2 name:quantity}


## Every kind of thing, as a relation of its own
#
# **Sean, 2026-09-17**, looking at a deposit, an extractor and a labor in one territory: *I notice
# that in territory-1, we have a deposit, an extractor, and a food. Yet the way we specify this is
# different.* **It was: two of them had their kind in the schema and one had it in a column.** A
# kind is a relation now, and `residency` and `thing` are both gone.
#
# **This is `spec/data/carries.4x` said in the notation** - a kind and the traits it carries is a
# relation and its columns.

{column id:76 relation:scout seq:1 name:where}
{column id:77 relation:scout seq:2 name:quantity}
{column id:78 relation:labor seq:1 name:where}
{column id:79 relation:labor seq:2 name:quantity}
{column id:80 relation:metal seq:1 name:where}
{column id:81 relation:metal seq:2 name:quantity}
{column id:82 relation:food seq:1 name:where}
{column id:83 relation:food seq:2 name:quantity}


## How many times a thing may still act
#
# **Sean, 2026-09-17**, on an extractor that can be worked as often as there is labor for it:
# *rediness is going to end up being a number of times we can do something rather than whether we
# have done something.* **So it is a quantity, and a quantity is a relation's.**
#
# **An allowance is its own counted relation rather than a column on the thing.** A column would
# have meant going from `working:3` to `working:2`, which is arithmetic on a named column and
# something the engine cannot do; a quantity is arithmetic it already does, and running out is the
# refusal it already gives. **What that gives up is which extractor was worked** - and two
# extractors with one work each cannot be told from two extractors sharing two works by anything
# the game can ask, because extractors are fungible.
#
# **One relation per kind of readiness**, named for the activity, which is what `spec/data/traits.4x`
# does with `working`, `moving`, `laboring` and `bearing`. Each is keyed like the thing that has
# it, so `{limit ...}` says an allowance cannot exceed the things holding it.

{column id:84 relation:working seq:1 name:where}
{column id:85 relation:working seq:2 name:what}
{column id:86 relation:working seq:3 name:quantity}


## What a place has room for, and what each kind takes up
#
# **Sean, 2026-09-17**: *I am thinking of inventing a resource that vehicles take up and having a
# certain limit per territory that is the same across all territories. A lot of real time strategy
# games do this, and I know the board game twilight imperium does this as well.*
#
# **The allowance is a constant and the rate is per kind**, which is the half `spec/data/limit.4x`
# cannot write: `{limit container:territory contained:ark n:2}` counts arks, and a transport worth
# two scouts has nowhere to be said.
#
# **A pool names the kind of place it is an allowance of**, and a drawing kind's place is whichever
# of its columns points at that - so the engine finds it rather than knowing the game calls it
# `where`.

{column id:87 relation:supply seq:1 name:id}
{column id:88 relation:supply seq:2 name:name}
{column id:89 relation:supply seq:3 name:per}

{column id:90 relation:provides seq:1 name:kind}
{column id:91 relation:provides seq:2 name:what}
{column id:92 relation:provides seq:3 name:quantity}

{column id:93 relation:consumes seq:1 name:kind}
{column id:94 relation:consumes seq:2 name:what}
{column id:95 relation:consumes seq:3 name:quantity}

{column id:96 relation:transport seq:1 name:where}
{column id:97 relation:transport seq:2 name:quantity}


