## Which relations are the game's state
#
# **This is what bounds the comparison.** `expected.4x` states the state after, and a diff has to
# know which relations to look at - without this it would compare the rule and the command too.

{state id:1 relation:territory}
{state id:3 relation:adjacency}
{state id:5 relation:deposit}
{state id:6 relation:extractor}
{state id:7 relation:scout}
{state id:8 relation:labor}
{state id:9 relation:metal}
{state id:10 relation:food}
{state id:11 relation:working}
{state id:12 relation:transport}
{state id:13 relation:provides}
{state id:14 relation:consumes}


## Which relations are families, and who belongs
#
# **A `unit` may be moved and a `resource` may be extracted**, and those are the two sets any rule
# here ranges over. **`move` is typed `of:unit`**, so a deposit is not offered to it because the
# game says a deposit is not a unit - rather than because `move`'s add clause happens to leave a
# column unbound, which is how it would have been excluded without this.

{family relation:unit}
{family relation:resource}

{member kind:scout family:unit}
{member kind:transport family:unit}
{member kind:metal family:resource}
{member kind:food family:resource}


## What a deposit's density is, and what a deposit holds
#
# **A density is a fact about a deposit and not part of what a deposit is.** Sean, 2026-09-17,
# ruling out the alternative: a territory may have deposits of two grades, or the notation may be
# uniform and minimal, and it cannot have both - *we are dropping (2)*. `spec/planet.md` asks for
# one: *For each resource, a territory has capacity for some number of extractors, and a density
# that each of them yields.*
#
# **So a second grade is two rows with one key and the structure refuses it**, which is the whole
# reason the column is marked: with density in the key it would be a legal state nothing could
# name.

{attribute column:53 relation:deposit}


# **An extractor stands in a deposit, so there cannot be more of them than there are deposits.**
# Sean: *We can't place an extractor if there are no available deposits.* No rule says so - every
# rule already refuses the world it would leave, so this binds rules that do not exist yet.

{limit held:extractor by:deposit}
{limit held:working by:extractor}


## What a supply is, and what it is measured in
#
# **How much is provided and how much is consumed are rows of the world**, stated in each test's
# `given` - Sean, 2026-09-17: *there are certain things I always want to see in tests because I
# need to compute the tests in my head.* **A layer that kept them out of a scenario would be a
# layer hiding what the test is about**, and he ruled that comprehension wins where the two
# conflict.
#
# **This declares the supply and nothing else.** `per` says what it is measured in, so the check
# does not have to work out which relation every provider and consumer has in common.

{supply id:1 name:berth per:territory}


## The three roles a clause can have
#
# **The engine branches on the `name`, not on the id.** An id is arbitrary, so a rule that says
# `role:2` means nothing until the row is looked up - which makes `role.name` load-bearing where
# every other `name` is decoration.

{role id:1 name:require}
{role id:2 name:remove}
{role id:3 name:add}
